  /* ============================ UNIT 6 ============================ */
  U['6'] = {
    title: 'Consequences of Industrialization',
    topics: [ {id:'6.1', name:'Rationales for Imperialism'}, {id:'6.2', name:'State Expansion'}, {id:'6.3', name:'Indigenous Responses'}, {id:'6.4', name:'Global Economic Development'}, {id:'6.5', name:'Economic Imperialism'}, {id:'6.6', name:'Causes of Migration'}, {id:'6.7', name:'Effects of Migration'}, {id:'6.8', name:'Causation in the Imperial Age'} ],
    questions: {
"6.1":[
  {s:"Take up the White Man's burden— / Send forth the best ye breed— / Go bind your sons to exile / To serve your captives' need; / To wait in heavy harness, / On fluttered folk and wild— / Your new-caught, sullen peoples, / Half-devil and half-child.",sl:"Rudyard Kipling, 'The White Man's Burden,' 1899",q:"Kipling's poem most directly reflects which ideology used to justify European and American imperialism in the late nineteenth century?",o:["Scientific racism and Social Darwinism, which depicted colonized peoples as racially inferior and in need of European guidance","The economic argument that colonial territories provided essential markets for surplus industrial goods","The strategic argument that controlling overseas territories was necessary for national defense","The religious argument that Christian missionaries had a duty to convert non-Christian populations"],c:0,e:"Kipling's language — 'half-devil and half-child,' 'sullen peoples' — reflects the Social Darwinist and scientific racist ideology that portrayed colonized peoples as racially and culturally inferior, requiring European 'civilizing' guidance. This paternalistic racism became a core justification for imperialism across the late 19th century, deployed alongside but distinct from purely economic or religious arguments."},
  {s:"The superior races have a right, because they have a duty. They have the duty to civilize the inferior races... Colonial policy is the daughter of industrial policy. Is it possible to say that in Europe — in the competitive scramble of so many rivals — we can afford not to export our goods abroad and not to seek investment opportunities for our capital? The answer is no.",sl:"Jules Ferry, French Prime Minister, speech defending French colonial expansion, 1884",q:"Ferry's speech illustrates which combination of factors driving European imperialism in the late nineteenth century?",o:["Religious conversion missions combined with humanitarian concern for colonized populations","Military security needs combined with opposition to rival powers' expansionism","Economic motivations linked to industrialization combined with an ideological belief in European cultural superiority","Diplomatic pressure from international organizations combined with domestic political opposition"],c:2,e:"Ferry explicitly links two central pillars of imperial justification: the economic need for markets and investment opportunities (a direct product of industrialization's surplus production capacity) and the racial-civilizational ideology that portrayed conquest as a 'duty' of 'superior races.' Both arguments were widely used across European imperial powers in the 1880s–1900s."},
  {s:"Social Darwinism took Darwin's insights about natural selection and applied them to human societies and nations. Herbert Spencer, who coined the phrase 'survival of the fittest,' argued that competition between nations and races naturally resulted in the rise of superior peoples. Imperialists used this framework to argue that the conquest of weaker peoples was not merely justified — it was the natural and inevitable unfolding of human progress.",sl:"Secondary source summary of Social Darwinist ideology, c. 1880s",q:"Social Darwinism differed from Charles Darwin's actual scientific theory most significantly in that it",o:["Rejected the idea that species could change over time through competition and natural selection","Applied biological concepts to human societies and nations to justify conquest and racial hierarchy","Argued that all human civilizations followed an identical path of evolutionary progress","Claimed that technological development determined which nations would survive international competition"],c:1,e:"Darwin's theory addressed biological evolution in plants and animals. Social Darwinism misapplied these biological concepts to human societies, nations, and races — using 'survival of the fittest' to justify imperial conquest and racial hierarchy. This misapplication was not scientific but ideological, giving a veneer of scientific legitimacy to policies that served imperial economic and political interests."}
],
"6.2":[
  {s:"The general act of the conference hereby establishes freedom of commerce throughout the Congo Basin... Any new occupation of territory on the coasts of Africa must be notified to the signatory powers. The principle of effective occupation is hereby established: a power claiming territory must demonstrate actual governance.",sl:"General Act of the Berlin Conference, February 26, 1885",q:"The Berlin Conference of 1884–85 most directly illustrates which aspect of European imperialism?",o:["European powers competed militarily for African territory, with victory in battle determining colonial borders","African rulers participated in negotiations that established borders protecting their existing territories","European powers collectively organized the partition of Africa without African participation, treating the continent as available for European acquisition","The United States played a leading role in mediating disputes between European colonial powers"],c:2,e:"The Berlin Conference is the paradigmatic example of European powers treating Africa as open for claiming. Representatives of 14 European nations and the United States met in Berlin to regulate the Scramble for Africa — establishing rules for how European powers could claim territory. No African rulers were invited or consulted. The result was the partition of roughly 80% of Africa into European colonies by 1900."},
  {s:"Japan has opened its mines, built railways, constructed ships, established telegraph systems, and reorganized its army and navy on European models — all within thirty years. The transformation is not mere imitation; it is the deliberate adoption of Western techniques to preserve Japanese independence. The nation that modernizes fastest will not be colonized.",sl:"Secondary source paraphrase of Meiji-era Japanese government strategy, c. 1890",q:"Japan's Meiji-era modernization most directly challenges which assumption often made about 19th-century imperialism?",o:["Western-style industrialization and modernization could only succeed in societies with existing Protestant Christian traditions","Non-Western nations could industrialize, build modern military forces, and resist colonization by selectively adopting Western technology","The Japanese government's authoritarian structure made industrialization faster than in European democracies","Colonization by Western powers was necessary for Asian nations to develop industrial economies"],c:1,e:"Japan's Meiji Restoration (1868) represents the most successful case of a non-Western nation adopting Western industrial and military technology to avoid colonization. By 1905, Japan defeated Russia — the first time a non-Western nation defeated a European power in modern warfare. Japan then itself became an imperial power, annexing Taiwan (1895) and Korea (1910), demonstrating that imperialism was not uniquely European."},
  {s:"The Government of India Act of 1858 transferred sovereignty over India from the British East India Company to the British Crown. Queen Victoria was proclaimed Empress of India in 1877. The Colonial Secretary noted: 'We have 300 million subjects who regard our rule as the guarantee of peace, order, and prosperity they could not provide for themselves.'",sl:"Secondary source summary of British Crown assumption of Indian governance, 1858",q:"The transfer of authority over India from the East India Company to the British Crown in 1858 most directly resulted from",o:["Indian nationalist demands for greater self-governance within the British imperial system","The British government's desire to more efficiently extract revenue from Indian territories","The Indian Rebellion of 1857, which demonstrated that corporate governance was inadequate to maintain British control","International diplomatic pressure from European rivals seeking to limit British expansion in Asia"],c:2,e:"The Indian Rebellion of 1857 — triggered by cultural insensitivity, economic exploitation, and the Enfield rifle cartridge controversy — forced Britain to dramatically reconsider its governance model. The East India Company's rule had proven unable to maintain order; Crown rule promised more direct, coordinated imperial governance. The colonial secretary's statement also exemplifies the civilizing mission ideology used to justify continued British control."}
],
"6.3":[
  {s:"The cartridges for the new Enfield rifle were rumored to be greased with pig and cow fat — forbidden to Muslims and Hindus respectively. The sepoys refused to use them. But this was only the spark. Behind it lay decades of resentment: the annexation of native states, the abolition of customs missionaries condemned as 'barbaric,' the replacement of Persian with English as the official administrative language. In May 1857, the rebellion had begun.",sl:"Secondary source narrative of the Indian Rebellion of 1857",q:"The Indian Rebellion of 1857 is best understood as",o:["A coordinated nationalist independence movement seeking to establish a unified independent Indian state","A mutiny sparked solely by religious offense over rifle cartridges, with no broader political causes","A response to multiple accumulated grievances against British rule, triggered by a specific cultural offense","A communist-inspired uprising against British capitalist exploitation of Indian workers"],c:2,e:"The cartridge controversy was the trigger, but historians emphasize the accumulation of grievances: loss of land through the Doctrine of Lapse, suppression of Indian customs, displacement of traditional elites, economic exploitation, and cultural humiliation. The rebellion was not a unified nationalist movement — it lacked coordination and a unified leadership — but it drew on broad resentment across northern India and represented the most significant challenge to British rule before the 20th-century nationalist movement."},
  {s:"Emperor Menelik II of Ethiopia, learning of the Italian advance, mobilized approximately 100,000 soldiers — including both traditional warriors and troops equipped with modern rifles and artillery purchased from France and Russia. At Adwa on March 1, 1896, Ethiopian forces surrounded and destroyed an Italian army of 18,000 men. Italy was forced to sign the Treaty of Addis Ababa, recognizing Ethiopia's full sovereignty.",sl:"Secondary source summary of the Battle of Adwa, 1896",q:"The Ethiopian victory at Adwa in 1896 was most significant because it",o:["Demonstrated that African kingdoms could defeat European armies through superior strategy and access to modern weapons, inspiring anti-colonial movements","Forced all European colonial powers to immediately abandon their territorial claims in Africa","Led directly to organized nationalist resistance across all of sub-Saharan Africa","Proved that African kingdoms could successfully incorporate European settlers into their political systems"],c:0,e:"Adwa was the only complete African military defeat of a European colonial army during the Scramble for Africa, and Ethiopia remained one of only two African nations never colonized (alongside Liberia). The battle became a powerful symbol across Africa and the African diaspora of the possibility of successful resistance. Menelik's military modernization — importing European weapons while maintaining Ethiopian political independence — became a model for later anti-colonial strategists."},
  {s:"The Qing court was divided. Some officials argued for immediate armed resistance to Western demands; others counseled accommodation. The 'Self-Strengthening Movement' sought to adopt Western military technology without altering Chinese political or cultural institutions. As one official argued: 'Chinese learning for the essence; Western learning for practical use.'",sl:"Secondary source summary of late Qing dynasty response to Western pressure, c. 1870s",q:"The Chinese Self-Strengthening Movement represents which type of indigenous response to imperialism?",o:["Armed resistance that sought to expel all Western presence from Chinese territory","Complete cultural assimilation, adopting both Western technology and Western political institutions","Selective modernization — using Western technology while preserving traditional Chinese political and cultural structures","Revolutionary overthrow of the Qing dynasty to establish a Western-style republic"],c:2,e:"The Self-Strengthening Movement (c. 1861–1895) represents selective modernization in response to imperialism — accepting Western military and industrial technology while rejecting Western political institutions and cultural values. The formula 'Chinese learning for the essence, Western learning for practical use' reflected elite anxiety about losing Chinese identity while recognizing the need to modernize military capacity. The movement ultimately failed — China's defeat in the 1894–95 Sino-Japanese War exposed the limits of technological modernization without political reform."}
],
"6.4":[
  {s:"By 1900, over 200,000 miles of railroad track spanned the globe. The railroad transformed the economics of empire: raw materials that once took weeks to reach a coastal port arrived within hours. The interior of continents — Africa, Asia, Latin America — was opened to commercial exploitation. The railroads transported cotton, rubber, and minerals outward; they carried manufactured goods inward.",sl:"Secondary source summary of railroad imperialism, c. 1900",q:"The global expansion of railroads in the nineteenth century most directly contributed to",o:["The economic independence of African and Asian societies by providing infrastructure for domestic trade","The integration of colonial territories into a global economy that extracted raw materials and supplied manufactured goods, deepening economic inequality","The spread of democratic governance by enabling faster communication between colonial governments and local populations","The decline of European industrial production as cheaper goods from colonial territories undercut domestic manufacturing"],c:1,e:"Railroads were built primarily to extract resources from colonial interiors to coastal ports for export, not to connect internal markets for domestic economic development. This 'export enclave' pattern meant railroads served colonial economic priorities, not local development needs. The result was structural economic dependency: colonies supplied raw materials and consumed manufactured imports, a pattern that persisted long after formal independence."},
  {s:"The British cotton textile industry consumed 80% of Egypt's raw cotton by 1880. Egyptian farmers who once grew food were shifted to cotton monoculture — profitable in good years, catastrophic when British mills reduced orders. When the global cotton price fell in 1873, Egyptian farmers faced bankruptcy while British manufacturers who bought their cotton at lower prices increased profits.",sl:"Secondary source analysis of Egypt's cotton economy under informal British influence, c. 1880",q:"The Egyptian cotton economy described in this passage best illustrates which pattern of economic imperialism?",o:["European industrial nations deliberately suppressed colonial agricultural production to prevent competition with their own farms","Colonial economies were restructured to serve the needs of industrial metropolitan economies, making colonized peoples dependent on global commodity prices they could not control","Free trade policies allowed Egyptian farmers to accumulate capital and invest in industrial development","British economic policy in Egypt was driven primarily by humanitarian concern for Egyptian agricultural workers"],c:1,e:"Egypt's transformation into a cotton monoculture exemplifies how imperialism restructured colonial economies to serve metropolitan industrial needs. Egyptian farmers lost food security and economic independence; their livelihoods became tied to global commodity prices determined by markets in Liverpool and Manchester, not Cairo. This 'dependent development' — growth that benefited colonial powers while creating vulnerability in colonized territories — became a standard critique of imperialism and, later, of neocolonialism."}
],
"6.5":[
  {s:"By the Treaty of Nanjing (1842), China ceded Hong Kong island to Britain. China was required to open five 'treaty ports' to British trade and residence. British citizens accused of crimes in China would be tried under British law in British courts — extraterritoriality. Britain was granted 'most-favored nation' status, ensuring it would receive any trade concession China granted to any other country. China paid an indemnity of 21 million silver dollars.",sl:"Secondary source summary of the Treaty of Nanjing, 1842",q:"The Treaty of Nanjing (1842) most directly illustrates which aspect of China's relationship with Western imperial powers in the nineteenth century?",o:["China's voluntary decision to open its economy to free trade in exchange for Western military protection","The imposition of unequal treaties that granted Western nations economic and legal privileges violating Chinese sovereignty","A negotiated compromise that balanced Chinese and British economic interests in the region","The failure of Western nations to establish meaningful commercial relationships with China"],c:1,e:"The Treaty of Nanjing established the template for what the Chinese called 'unequal treaties' — imposed by military force after China's defeat in the First Opium War, not negotiated between equals. Extraterritoriality, most-favored nation status, and forced treaty ports all represented direct violations of Chinese sovereignty. These treaties became the foundation of the 'Century of Humiliation' narrative that shaped Chinese politics through the 20th century."},
  {s:"In June 1900, Chinese forces besieged the foreign legation quarter in Beijing for 55 days. The siege was lifted by an eight-nation international military force. The Boxer Protocol of 1901 required China to pay an indemnity of 450 million taels — more than four times China's annual government revenue. China was prohibited from importing arms. Foreign troops were permanently stationed in Beijing.",sl:"Secondary source summary of the Boxer Rebellion and Protocol, 1900–01",q:"The Boxer Uprising and the subsequent Boxer Protocol most directly demonstrates",o:["China's successful use of armed resistance to restore territorial sovereignty and limit Western privileges","The failure of accommodation strategies — China's willingness to grant trade privileges only increased foreign demands","That armed resistance deepened foreign control and financial burdens while failing to expel foreign powers","The effectiveness of the Qing government's military modernization in resisting foreign intervention"],c:2,e:"The Boxer Uprising seemed to represent a powerful armed response to imperialism, especially when the Qing government formally declared war on the foreign powers. But the eight-nation alliance suppressed the rebellion militarily, and the Boxer Protocol imposed humiliating terms that further limited Chinese sovereignty and created massive debt. The uprising accelerated the decline of the Qing dynasty rather than restoring Chinese autonomy — illustrating how armed resistance could sometimes worsen the conditions that inspired it."}
],
"6.6":[
  {s:"Between 1848 and 1852, more than one million Irish people emigrated to the United States, fleeing the potato famine that killed approximately one million more. They arrived as unskilled laborers concentrated in northeastern cities. By the 1860s, Irish immigrants provided labor for the eastern transcontinental railroad while Chinese workers — recruited directly from Guangdong province — built the western half.",sl:"Secondary source summary of mid-19th century labor migration to the United States",q:"The migration patterns described in this passage are best explained by which combination of push and pull factors?",o:["Religious persecution in Ireland and China that drove migrants toward the religious freedom offered by the United States","Economic catastrophe in sending countries combined with massive labor demand in a rapidly industrializing destination economy","Political repression by governments in Ireland and China that drove political refugees to seek asylum in the United States","Active U.S. government recruitment programs that transported workers from Ireland and China to fill railroad shortages"],c:1,e:"Push-pull analysis is the standard framework for 19th-century migration. Push factors: the Irish famine (1845–52) and economic desperation in Guangdong province. Pull factors: American industrial and infrastructure expansion creating massive unskilled labor demand, particularly for dangerous, low-wage work European-American workers refused. The transcontinental railroad exemplifies this pattern — Chinese laborers built the more difficult Sierra Nevada sections under brutal conditions for wages below white workers."},
  {s:"Following British abolition of slavery in 1833, plantation owners in the Caribbean, South Africa, and Southeast Asia faced labor shortages. Britain established indentured servitude: laborers from India, China, and elsewhere signed contracts binding them to five-year terms in exchange for transportation, wages, and eventual return passage. Between 1834 and 1920, over 1.5 million Indians traveled to Fiji, Trinidad, Guyana, Mauritius, and South Africa under these contracts.",sl:"Secondary source summary of the indentured labor system, 19th century",q:"The indentured labor system described in this passage most directly illustrates which historical continuity?",o:["The persistence of coerced and exploitative labor systems following the formal abolition of chattel slavery","Britain's commitment to providing equal economic opportunities to workers across its empire","The genuinely voluntary character of labor migration in the British Empire following abolition","The decline of plantation agriculture in the British Empire following emancipation"],c:0,e:"Indentured servitude is often described as a 'new system of slavery.' While technically voluntary (contracts were signed), conditions were coercive: laborers were often deceived about conditions, could not break contracts, and faced criminal penalties for non-compliance. The system maintained the plantation economy while technically complying with abolition. It represents a continuity: labor coercion persisted in modified form, using debt, contract, and criminal law to bind workers to plantations after chattel slavery was abolished."}
],
"6.7":[
  {s:"'The Chinese must go!' cried Denis Kearney at San Francisco rally after rally. In 1882, the United States Congress passed the Chinese Exclusion Act, prohibiting the immigration of Chinese laborers for ten years — later extended indefinitely. It was the first major federal law restricting immigration by nationality and race. Courts ruled that Chinese already in the United States could not become naturalized citizens.",sl:"Secondary source summary of the Chinese Exclusion Act, 1882",q:"The Chinese Exclusion Act (1882) most directly resulted from which development?",o:["Evidence that Chinese immigrants were statistically more likely to commit crimes than other immigrant groups","Chinese government protests that emigration was depleting China of needed agricultural workers","Nativist backlash against Chinese immigration, driven by economic competition, racial prejudice, and fears about cultural change","The failure of Chinese immigrants to assimilate into American society through intermarriage and cultural adoption"],c:2,e:"The Chinese Exclusion Act was the product of nativist politics, not evidence-based policy. Economic anxiety (particularly among white workers competing with Chinese laborers in mining and railroad construction), racial ideology, and political opportunism combined to produce the first racially-based federal immigration restriction. This was part of a global pattern: Australia, Canada, and New Zealand enacted similar restrictions — reflecting how migration generated nativist political reactions across receiving countries."},
  {s:"By 1900, San Francisco's Chinatown was the largest concentration of ethnic Chinese outside Asia. Indian communities in Natal, Trinidad, and Fiji maintained Hindu temples, practiced Diwali, and preserved cultural traditions across generations. In Port of Spain, Chinese shopkeepers sold goods to Afro-Caribbean customers using Creole phrases mixed with Cantonese. Diaspora communities simultaneously preserved home cultures and created hybrid new ones.",sl:"Secondary source description of 19th-century diaspora communities",q:"The patterns of cultural preservation and adaptation described in this passage most directly illustrate which effect of late nineteenth-century migration?",o:["The rapid and complete assimilation of immigrant communities into the dominant cultures of receiving countries","The creation of diaspora communities that preserved home cultures while also developing new hybrid cultural forms shaped by their new environments","The failure of migrant communities to establish permanent settlements due to intentions to return home","The cultural destruction of immigrant communities through forced assimilation policies in receiving countries"],c:1,e:"Diaspora communities in the late 19th century demonstrated a consistent pattern: cultural retention (language, religion, food, festivals, family structures) alongside cultural adaptation (adopting local languages, intermarrying, developing creole cultures). Chinatowns, Indian-Caribbean communities, and South Asian settlements in Africa all created hybrid cultural identities — neither fully preserving the original nor fully assimilating into the new. This cultural syncretism became a defining feature of the globalized world that imperialism and migration created."}
],
"6.8":[
  {s:"It was not empire for empire's sake that drove European expansion in the late nineteenth century. It was the factory. The steam engine required coal; the textile mill required cotton; the metallurgical plant required iron ore. European industry had outgrown European markets — and so European industrialists looked outward. The map of empire is, in its essential logic, a map of European industrial demand.",sl:"Secondary source historical analysis connecting industrialization to imperialism, c. 1900",q:"This passage most directly supports which argument about the relationship between industrialization and imperialism?",o:["European imperialism was primarily motivated by religious missionary activity that accompanied industrial expansion","Industrial development in Europe created economic incentives — for raw materials, markets, and investment opportunities — that drove territorial expansion","European workers supported imperialism because colonial labor competition raised their wages","The industrialization of colonial territories was the primary goal of European imperial expansion"],c:1,e:"This argument — that industrialization created the economic conditions that made imperialism necessary for continued growth — is the core of economic theories of imperialism (associated with J.A. Hobson and later Lenin). Surplus capital needing investment, industrial demand for raw materials European territories couldn't supply, and saturated domestic markets all pushed industrializing nations toward colonial expansion. This economic causation argument is central to AP causation questions about the Imperial Age."},
  {s:"The Portuguese arrived in West Africa in the 1440s seeking gold and enslaved people. Four hundred years later, the British arrived in the same regions seeking rubber, cocoa, and labor. The tools differed — steamships replaced sailing vessels, rifles replaced matchlocks — but the underlying relationship showed a disturbing continuity: extraction of resources and labor for European benefit, with African political and economic autonomy as the price.",sl:"Secondary source comparative analysis of European-African interactions, 1440s–1880s",q:"The comparison drawn in this passage is best used to support which historical claim?",o:["There was no meaningful difference between early modern European imperialism and nineteenth-century colonialism","African resistance to European contact was equally ineffective across both periods","The economic relationship of resource extraction and labor exploitation showed continuity from the early modern period to the era of New Imperialism, despite changing technologies","Portuguese colonialism was more economically damaging to Africa than British colonialism"],c:2,e:"The AP skill of continuity-and-change requires students to identify what persists across time periods. This passage argues that the fundamental economic relationship — European extraction of African resources and labor — showed continuity from the early modern slave trade to 19th-century colonialism, even as technologies, political structures, and ideological justifications changed. This continuity argument is the kind of complexity the AP exam rewards — it neither collapses the two eras into one nor treats them as entirely separate."}
]
}
  };

